WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:04.320
Well, there they are, sheriff; 
Mr. Creeps and his partner Mr. Crawls.

00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.380
I've been asked to make a video on plot twists 
since the beginning of this channel.

00:00:08.380 --> 00:00:12.780
But I was always hesitant to do it because I didn't want to spoil a bunch of major plot twists.

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:17.720
I stood by this principle until I came across an interesting study conducted by UC San Diego.

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:23.180
Researchers had one group of participants read several famous books, 
after the researchers spoiled their endings.

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:25.320
While another group
read the books normally.

00:00:25.320 --> 00:00:28.140
After reading,
each group noted how much they liked each book.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:34.420
Based on the results, it turns out that if the ending is spoiled to you before you read a book, you actually end up liking the book more.

00:00:34.420 --> 00:00:38.160
Not only do I think this study perfectly explains what makes a plot twist great,

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:41.390
I also like it because it helps me justify spoiling some movies.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:43.580
I'll have to spoil "Now You See Me" 
and "Primal Fear".

00:00:43.580 --> 00:00:46.180
With "The Prestige" having 
only very minor spoilers.

00:00:46.180 --> 00:00:48.800
If you haven't seen one, 
consider what the study says,

00:00:48.800 --> 00:00:51.660
and use this video as an opportunity
to recreate the experiment.

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.080
So let's get spoiling!

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:56.060
"We're going to follow those dirty feet
and find that phantom!"

00:00:56.060 --> 00:01:00.600
The common definition of a plot twist is:
A totally unexpected outcome to a story.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.880
In  "Now You See Me"  Dylan Rhodes is an FBI agent, trying to track down the four Horsemen.

00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:09.120
But in a shocking twist, it turns out he was the fifth horseman all along.

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.980
While I do think the rest of the movie is pretty fun, 
there is one key reason that this plot twist doesn't really work.

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:17.793
Alfred Hitchcock: 
"Now you and I sit in here,

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:19.560
suddenly a bomb goes off!"

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:24.460
Thaddeus Bradley: 
And... the FBI got past them. Not once, not twice, but consistently.

00:01:24.460 --> 00:01:26.920
Almost as if they were only in ...

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:29.120
* Silence *

00:01:29.300 --> 00:01:34.180
Hitchcock: "Once the audience had watched this scene. 5 or 10 seconds of shock.

00:01:35.280 --> 00:01:37.120
* Intro to Scooby-Doo *

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:40.400
"Scooby Dooby Doo, 
where are you?"

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:43.440
"We've got some work to do now!"
* Scooby gulps.*

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.480
Even though the original shows 
are pretty simple and predictable,

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:50.100
Scooby-Doo actually adheres 
to an important principle of good plot twists:

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:54.300
The final payoff always follows the established logic 
at the beginning of each episode

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:57.900
"They were trying to scare all the heirs off the island."

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:02.600
"Then no one would spend the night and they would keep control of the fortune"

00:02:02.600 --> 00:02:06.600
We get just enough information in the beginning, 
so that when the twist does happen, it's satisfying.

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:09.820
Since there was always that looming feeling 
that you know who did it.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:14.600
"Creeps and Crawls. 
They sure picked the right lawyers for this job!"

00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:19.260
The "Now You See Me" twist, has this element missing. 
It follows very little established logic.

00:02:19.260 --> 00:02:23.320
The complete rewriting of the character, 
from a clumsy FBI agent to a cunning genius

00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:26.060
means there was really no way
to see this plot twist coming.

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:29.640
And that is why its not very satisfying,
and relies too heavily on shock.

00:02:29.640 --> 00:02:33.820
"The Prestige" is the perfect balance,
 between the 
shock value of the "Now You See Me" twist,

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.520
and the predictable, 
but logical twists of Scooby-Doo.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.300
Twenty minutes into the movie, 
we've technically been told enough
to predict the plot twist.

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:42.880
But Christopher Nolan 
leaves just enough information out,

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:46.580
to make the ending an amazing shock, 
that still follows established logic.

00:02:46.580 --> 00:02:50.280
Hitchcock: 
"Now, we do the scene over again, 
but we tell the audience:

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:54.076
there's a bomb underneath this table, 
and it's gonna go off in five minutes."

00:02:54.080 --> 00:02:57.420
Off-shot: 
"You're looking for the secret, but you won't find it."

00:02:57.420 --> 00:03:01.000
"Because of course, 
you're not REALLY looking"

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:05.280
The best plot twist is the one that can create the biggest surprise,
without ruining the established logic.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:08.980
Because at the end of the day, 
the twist in "Now You See Me" 
isn't actually that satisfying.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.600
It's a complete rewrite of his character for a cheap surprise.

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:17.659
It's not particularly interesting 
to change 100% of a character's personality, 
that has been built up throughout the whole movie.

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:20.640
So, how do you do this in a surprising, but also entertaining way?

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.320
In "Primal Fear", 
the plot twist is similar,
but with one key difference.

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:27.740
In the movie, an altar boy is accused of murdering an archbishop.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:30.651
The lawyer gets him off: 
"not guilty by reason of insanity",

00:03:30.651 --> 00:03:33.800
because the boy tests positive 
for multiple personality disorder.

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:38.200
The film ends with a final scene, 
before the altar boy is moved to a psychiatric hospital.

00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:41.520
Here he reveals
he faked it the whole time to get a short sentence.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:46.140
"There never was: 
'an Aaron', counsellor."

00:03:46.220 --> 00:03:51.360
It may seem like pure shock, but this ending is actually carefully woven into the logic of the story.

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.260
Throughout the movie 
we're not sure which personality he is. 
Or when he's about to change his identity.

00:03:55.260 --> 00:03:58.160
So we've always had this feeling 
that he wasn't who he says he was.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.020
But the shock is still fantastic,

00:04:00.020 --> 00:04:04.620
because we go from being on his side, 
to him tricking everybody in the story (including us),

00:04:04.620 --> 00:04:06.880
and switching to an antagonist at the very end.

00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.200
We got the information,
but it's still a surprise.

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:12.380
And it's what separates 
the good plot twists, from the bad ones.

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:18.560
Hitchcock: 
"In order to get suspense you provide the audience with a certain amount of information.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.160
Aaron: 
"Come on buddy, 
I thought you had it figured there at the end."

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:24.980
Hitchcock continues: 
"and leave the rest of it,
to their own imagination."

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:27.340
Aaron: "But to tell you the truth. I am glad you figured it,

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:30.440
because i have been dying to tell you! Ha!"

00:04:30.440 --> 00:04:34.940
Give them just enough to see it coming,
but not enough to expect it

00:04:34.940 --> 00:04:36.620
(Whispered): 
"I see dead people."

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:40.440
I think the best way to test a good plot twist is 
if the movie is re-watchable.

00:04:40.440 --> 00:04:43.280
Because once you know the twist ending, 
(if the ending is a good one).

00:04:43.300 --> 00:04:48.440
Then it's just as fun (if not more fun), 
to go back and see 
where they hint at that twist ending.

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:50.880
"Are you watching closely?"

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:55.760
And I think that's why the study shows:
how spoiling a book before reading it,
makes it more enjoyable.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:04:59.620
The suspense of the ending 
makes the path getting there 
that much more exciting.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:03.380
And maybe that's why lots of stories 
spoil the ending at the very beginning.

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:07.840
"And me! 
I'm the damn fool that shot him!"

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:11.120
Because as much as it's about the plot twists, 
it's about HOW you get there.

00:05:11.120 --> 00:05:13.780
So feel free to watch something, 
even if you know how it ends.

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.740
Because who knows? 
Maybe you'll like it even more.

00:05:15.740 --> 00:05:21.280
"Then bring the dog and come in. 
The others are waiting inside."

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:23.580
* dramatic music *

00:05:23.580 --> 00:05:25.040
* Scooby-Doo gulps *

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:27.440
Thanks for watching!

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:30.100
* Intro to Scooby-Doo *:
"Scooby Dooby Doo, where are you?"

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.460
Fading out:
"We've got some work to do now!"

